WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.860
Welcome to Module 5 of the
Non-Person Identity learning path:

2
00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:06.450
cut the privilege of service principals.

3
00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:08.971
This module is the first half of Gate 4,

4
00:00:08.971 --> 00:00:12.875
Least privilege, with Chapter
7 of the developer workbook.

5
00:00:12.875 --> 00:00:18.041
You'll sweep every service principal for
what it holds: its application permissions,

6
00:00:18.041 --> 00:00:22.838
its delegated grants, its directory roles,
and a managed identity's Azure roles.

7
00:00:22.838 --> 00:00:27.288
You'll flag the broad grants that have no
current exception, hand the privileged

8
00:00:27.288 --> 00:00:30.121
service principals to the
Privileged Access plan,

9
00:00:30.121 --> 00:00:32.212
and build the quarterly owner review.

10
00:00:32.212 --> 00:00:35.292
Then you'll turn the owners'
decisions into a plan,

11
00:00:35.292 --> 00:00:38.912
and apply it only after a second
person approves its hash.

12
00:00:38.912 --> 00:00:42.213
The code runs on your laptop
against a fictional estate.

13
00:00:42.213 --> 00:00:47.580
It's illustrative: tested against
fixtures, not run against a live tenant.

14
00:00:51.330 --> 00:00:52.943
Why does privilege need cutting?

15
00:00:52.943 --> 00:00:57.918
Because a workload can use its access
faster and more widely than any person.

16
00:00:57.918 --> 00:01:00.892
An application permission
gives an app everything

17
00:01:00.892 --> 00:01:03.243
its scope allows, across the whole tenant.

18
00:01:03.243 --> 00:01:06.404
And a permission like Directory
dot ReadWrite dot All,

19
00:01:06.404 --> 00:01:09.706
granted to make it work, stays
until someone takes it away.

20
00:01:09.706 --> 00:01:13.480
In this module you find those
grants and take them away safely.

21
00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:15.655
You sweep every service principal,

22
00:01:15.655 --> 00:01:19.930
flag the broad grants without a
current exception, and ask the owners.

23
00:01:19.930 --> 00:01:21.680
Two rules hold the whole time.

24
00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:25.630
Nothing is removed until a second
person approves the plan's hash.

25
00:01:25.630 --> 00:01:28.830
And the code removes
a grant, never an app.

26
00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:35.418
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

27
00:01:35.418 --> 00:01:37.805
which takes about sixty
minutes with the lab.

28
00:01:37.805 --> 00:01:41.236
You'll explain the difference
between an application permission

29
00:01:41.236 --> 00:01:44.542
and a delegated grant, and why the
type matters more than the name.

30
00:01:44.542 --> 00:01:48.967
You'll name what makes a grant broad,
and what a recorded exception must hold.

31
00:01:48.967 --> 00:01:52.627
You'll say which service principals
go to the Privileged Access plan,

32
00:01:52.627 --> 00:01:54.681
and why this chapter doesn't sweep them.

33
00:01:54.681 --> 00:01:58.211
And you'll run the sweep, read
the owner review and the plan,

34
00:01:58.211 --> 00:02:01.592
and watch the write path
refuse a plan nobody approved.

35
00:02:01.592 --> 00:02:06.199
Before you start, finish Module 1,
because the sweep reads its register,

36
00:02:06.199 --> 00:02:10.092
and Module 2, because the review
goes to the owners it checks.

37
00:02:10.092 --> 00:02:15.740
Lab 1 needs PowerShell 7.1 or
later with Pester 5, and no tenant.

38
00:02:19.490 --> 00:02:23.977
Unit 1 is the guide's Step 4,
sub-steps four a to four c.

39
00:02:23.977 --> 00:02:25.677
Four a is Graph permissions.

40
00:02:25.677 --> 00:02:30.582
Microsoft Learn's guidance is to prefer
delegated access where a user is present,

41
00:02:30.582 --> 00:02:33.510
because a delegated grant
gives the app only the

42
00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:36.365
intersection of the user's
rights and the scope.

43
00:02:36.365 --> 00:02:38.824
And judge each grant by
its type, not its name:

44
00:02:38.824 --> 00:02:43.743
the Microsoft Graph permissions reference
shows Mail dot ReadWrite and Mail dot Send

45
00:02:43.743 --> 00:02:48.115
as application permissions, with the
same names as their delegated versions,

46
00:02:48.115 --> 00:02:50.165
and the program rates them critical.

47
00:02:50.165 --> 00:02:54.733
Learn also says removing a permission
from the app registration doesn't revoke

48
00:02:54.733 --> 00:02:59.302
an existing grant, and that tokens already
issued stay valid for their lifetime.

49
00:02:59.302 --> 00:03:01.177
Four b is directory roles.

50
00:03:01.177 --> 00:03:04.658
Microsoft Learn's page on
privileged roles calls a service

51
00:03:04.658 --> 00:03:08.352
principal with a highly privileged
role a control-plane asset.

52
00:03:08.352 --> 00:03:11.173
Four c is Azure:
Microsoft's guidance is to

53
00:03:11.173 --> 00:03:13.828
scope a role to the resource it's used on.

54
00:03:13.828 --> 00:03:17.510
Four d and four e are Module 6's.

55
00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:23.918
Unit 2 starts with the
rule the code holds,

56
00:03:23.918 --> 00:03:26.810
which is the program's,
so it's design guidance.

57
00:03:26.810 --> 00:03:29.454
Owners review every grant every quarter,

58
00:03:29.454 --> 00:03:33.110
and the review closes only when
its findings are acted on.

59
00:03:33.110 --> 00:03:36.904
No broad permission stands
without a recorded exception,

60
00:03:36.904 --> 00:03:41.647
which names a justification, an approver,
an approval date and an end date.

61
00:03:41.647 --> 00:03:45.014
Where Microsoft Learn documents
a narrower replacement,

62
00:03:45.014 --> 00:03:47.609
such as Selected
permissions in SharePoint,

63
00:03:47.609 --> 00:03:52.448
or role-based access control for applications
in Exchange Online, that's the target.

64
00:03:52.448 --> 00:03:56.310
A managed identity's Azure roles
belong at the resource, because,

65
00:03:56.310 --> 00:03:59.515
as Microsoft Learn's Azure
role assignment steps say,

66
00:03:59.515 --> 00:04:03.960
a role at a subscription or management
group reaches everything under it.

67
00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:08.010
Privileged service principals are
handed to the Privileged Access plan.

68
00:04:08.010 --> 00:04:10.581
And the code removes a
grant, never the app:

69
00:04:10.581 --> 00:04:15.850
one app role assignment through the
resource, or one delegated grant narrowed.

70
00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:23.317
The sweep reads every service
principal in the register:

71
00:04:23.317 --> 00:04:28.537
its app role assignments on every resource,
not just Graph, its delegated grants,

72
00:04:28.537 --> 00:04:32.650
its directory roles, and for a
managed identity, its Azure roles.

73
00:04:32.650 --> 00:04:37.437
Then the check applies nine
rules, N P L P oh one to oh nine.

74
00:04:37.437 --> 00:04:40.853
Most rest on Microsoft Learn's
permission and role pages;

75
00:04:40.853 --> 00:04:45.650
the exception rules and the unnamed-role
rule are the program's design guidance.

76
00:04:45.650 --> 00:04:50.088
Oh one: a broad application permission
with no recorded exception.

77
00:04:50.088 --> 00:04:54.688
Oh two: an exception that ended,
or that's missing a justification,

78
00:04:54.688 --> 00:04:57.726
an approver, an approval
date or an end date.

79
00:04:57.726 --> 00:05:02.188
Oh three: a broad delegated
scope consented for all users.

80
00:05:02.188 --> 00:05:06.425
Oh four is subtle: a mail permission
still granted in Entra while its

81
00:05:06.425 --> 00:05:10.888
exception relies on Exchange's scoped
access, so the scope does nothing.

82
00:05:10.888 --> 00:05:14.187
Oh five is mail limited by
the legacy access policy.

83
00:05:14.187 --> 00:05:19.363
Oh six and oh seven grade a managed identity's
Azure role by how wide its scope is.

84
00:05:19.363 --> 00:05:21.324
Oh eight hands a privileged app off.

85
00:05:21.324 --> 00:05:24.240
And oh nine is a role nobody can name.

86
00:05:27.990 --> 00:05:30.765
Two pieces sit between
the check and the plan.

87
00:05:30.765 --> 00:05:32.327
First, the hand-off.

88
00:05:32.327 --> 00:05:37.041
Four escalation permissions let an app
grant itself more or act as others.

89
00:05:37.041 --> 00:05:41.395
The Microsoft Graph permissions reference
cautions that app role assignment

90
00:05:41.395 --> 00:05:45.090
read-write and role management
read-write can grant authorization,

91
00:05:45.090 --> 00:05:48.653
and that application read-write
lets an app act as other entities.

92
00:05:48.653 --> 00:05:53.031
And Microsoft Learn's guide to granting
permissions says only appropriate

93
00:05:53.031 --> 00:05:57.202
users should reach apps granted
delegated permission grant read-write.

94
00:05:57.202 --> 00:06:01.194
Any service principal holding
one, or holding a directory role,

95
00:06:01.194 --> 00:06:06.415
is handed off whole to the Privileged Access
plan, with everything it holds listed.

96
00:06:06.415 --> 00:06:10.152
It gets no finding here, so two
plans never disagree about it.

97
00:06:10.152 --> 00:06:12.477
Second, the quarterly owner review.

98
00:06:12.477 --> 00:06:15.059
It has one row per grant of every service

99
00:06:15.059 --> 00:06:17.953
principal that stays
here, sent to its owners.

100
00:06:17.953 --> 00:06:22.991
Under the program's design guidance,
only decisions dated this quarter count;

101
00:06:22.991 --> 00:06:27.478
an earlier answer shows as the last
decision, and the row is open again.

102
00:06:27.478 --> 00:06:30.864
The review closes when every
row is decided this quarter

103
00:06:30.864 --> 00:06:34.240
and nothing approved for
removal is still granted.

104
00:06:37.990 --> 00:06:40.802
Unit 3 is the plan and the write path.

105
00:06:40.802 --> 00:06:44.924
The plan has one action per broad
grant and per decided change,

106
00:06:44.924 --> 00:06:46.864
and only two kinds ever write.

107
00:06:46.864 --> 00:06:52.011
Remove app role removes an application
permission an owner decided to remove,

108
00:06:52.011 --> 00:06:55.652
but only once any named
replacement is already granted.

109
00:06:55.652 --> 00:07:00.677
Narrow grant shrinks a delegated grant to
a smaller set of scopes, never to nothing.

110
00:07:00.677 --> 00:07:03.760
Everything else is recorded
and sends nothing:

111
00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:07.948
waiting for a replacement or a
decision, kept under an exception,

112
00:07:07.948 --> 00:07:12.453
re-scoped by the owner in reviewed
infrastructure code, or handed off.

113
00:07:12.453 --> 00:07:14.678
The write path is a separate script.

114
00:07:14.678 --> 00:07:17.660
It runs only the plan
whose hash was approved,

115
00:07:17.660 --> 00:07:21.127
for this tenant and cloud,
no more than seven days old.

116
00:07:21.127 --> 00:07:23.753
It re-reads each target before writing.

117
00:07:23.753 --> 00:07:28.578
And it runs as a person with Privileged Role
Administrator, never an unattended app,

118
00:07:28.578 --> 00:07:32.355
because, as the Microsoft Graph
permissions reference cautions,

119
00:07:32.355 --> 00:07:35.852
the write permissions can
grant authorization themselves.

120
00:07:35.852 --> 00:07:39.320
The seven-day limit is the
program's design guidance.

121
00:07:43.070 --> 00:07:43.795
Try it.

122
00:07:43.795 --> 00:07:47.107
Lab 1 runs in the
non-person identity folder,

123
00:07:47.107 --> 00:07:50.420
in PowerShell 7, against
a fictional estate.

124
00:07:50.420 --> 00:07:55.508
First make Chapter 1's register, as in
Module 1, because the sweep reads it.

125
00:07:55.508 --> 00:08:00.583
Step one runs Learn's review script
against the stand-ins: ten tests pass.

126
00:08:00.583 --> 00:08:03.057
Step two lists what each task needs.

127
00:08:03.057 --> 00:08:06.321
The sweep holds three Graph
read permissions and Reader.

128
00:08:06.321 --> 00:08:09.059
The write task is separate,
and it's the one with

129
00:08:09.059 --> 00:08:12.158
app role assignment and
delegated grant read-write.

130
00:08:12.158 --> 00:08:16.233
Step three sweeps eleven service
principals and twenty grants:

131
00:08:16.233 --> 00:08:21.232
twelve application permissions, five
Azure roles and three delegated grants.

132
00:08:21.232 --> 00:08:24.667
Two are skipped, the agent
identity and its blueprint,

133
00:08:24.667 --> 00:08:29.195
which are Module 9's, and three are
handed to the Privileged Access plan.

134
00:08:29.195 --> 00:08:34.195
The owner review has fifteen rows,
six still open, so it isn't closed.

135
00:08:34.195 --> 00:08:37.407
And the plan has eleven
actions, three of them writes.

136
00:08:37.407 --> 00:08:38.950
Nothing is sent.

137
00:08:42.700 --> 00:08:46.800
Step four reads the findings by
rule, and every rule shows up.

138
00:08:46.800 --> 00:08:50.654
Oh one twice: the Mail Automation
app holds the broad directory

139
00:08:50.654 --> 00:08:54.063
write permission and mail
read-write with no exception.

140
00:08:54.063 --> 00:08:58.624
Oh two: HR Connector's exception
for full control of sites has ended.

141
00:08:58.624 --> 00:09:02.525
Oh three: Partner CRM
reads mail for all users.

142
00:09:02.525 --> 00:09:05.387
Oh four and oh five
are the two mail rules.

143
00:09:05.387 --> 00:09:09.580
Oh six twice and oh seven: a batch
machine's managed identity holds

144
00:09:09.580 --> 00:09:14.238
Azure roles at the subscription, a
management group, and a resource group.

145
00:09:14.238 --> 00:09:17.449
Oh nine: an app role the
resource doesn't publish.

146
00:09:17.449 --> 00:09:20.050
And oh eight three times, the hand-off.

147
00:09:20.050 --> 00:09:24.650
Build Pipeline holds role management
read-write, so it can grant itself more.

148
00:09:24.650 --> 00:09:28.313
Deploy Bot holds a directory
role and application read-write.

149
00:09:28.313 --> 00:09:31.400
And id deploy is Contributor
on a subscription.

150
00:09:31.400 --> 00:09:34.600
All three go to the
Privileged Access plan whole.

151
00:09:38.350 --> 00:09:41.625
Step five reads the open
rows of the owner review.

152
00:09:41.625 --> 00:09:42.762
There are six.

153
00:09:42.762 --> 00:09:46.405
One of them shows how the quarter
works: the batch machine's User

154
00:09:46.405 --> 00:09:49.976
dot Read dot All was kept on July
fifteenth, an earlier quarter.

155
00:09:49.976 --> 00:09:52.739
That answer doesn't count
in the fourth quarter,

156
00:09:52.739 --> 00:09:55.787
so the row is open again, and
the owner has to answer it.

157
00:09:55.787 --> 00:09:58.688
Step six reads the plan's eleven actions.

158
00:09:58.688 --> 00:09:59.612
Three write.

159
00:09:59.612 --> 00:10:01.718
Remove app role for HR Connector,

160
00:10:01.718 --> 00:10:07.099
whose full-control site permission can go
because its replacement, selected sites,

161
00:10:07.099 --> 00:10:11.700
is already granted; a removal that
names a replacement waits until it is.

162
00:10:11.700 --> 00:10:15.638
Remove app role for Mail Automation's
broad directory permission.

163
00:10:15.638 --> 00:10:19.187
And narrow grant for Partner
CRM's delegated grant.

164
00:10:19.187 --> 00:10:23.343
The other eight write nothing:
three handed off, the kept ones,

165
00:10:23.343 --> 00:10:28.520
the rows awaiting a decision, and an
Azure role for its owner to re-scope.

166
00:10:32.270 --> 00:10:35.470
Step seven dry-runs the plan
with its approved hash.

167
00:10:35.470 --> 00:10:37.333
It would send three requests.

168
00:10:37.333 --> 00:10:41.427
Two are deletes, and look where they
go: through the service principal

169
00:10:41.427 --> 00:10:45.307
ending in one oh, which is Microsoft
Graph's own service principal.

170
00:10:45.307 --> 00:10:48.268
The removal goes through
the resource, not the app,

171
00:10:48.268 --> 00:10:52.383
the way Microsoft Learn's Delete
app role assigned to reference says.

172
00:10:52.383 --> 00:10:55.771
The third is a patch that
narrows one delegated grant.

173
00:10:55.771 --> 00:10:59.533
Step eight applies the plan to
the fake, and three are sent.

174
00:10:59.533 --> 00:11:04.457
Then it tries a hash nobody approved,
all zeros, and the write path refuses:

175
00:11:04.457 --> 00:11:09.382
the plan's hash isn't the approved one,
so it won't run a plan nobody reviewed.

176
00:11:09.382 --> 00:11:14.396
Step nine runs every test: five
hundred thirteen pass, none fail.

177
00:11:14.396 --> 00:11:16.232
Then delete the output folder.

178
00:11:16.232 --> 00:11:20.330
The owner review names owners,
so don't leave it lying around.

179
00:11:24.080 --> 00:11:27.605
Lab 2 makes one real removal
in your own test tenant.

180
00:11:27.605 --> 00:11:32.392
You need a test tenant in the worldwide
or G C C cloud and a test subscription,

181
00:11:32.392 --> 00:11:35.403
never production, with
a test app registration

182
00:11:35.403 --> 00:11:38.105
and a test user-assigned managed identity.

183
00:11:38.105 --> 00:11:41.408
A reading app holds the three
read permissions and Reader,

184
00:11:41.408 --> 00:11:45.055
and a person with Privileged Role
Administrator does the write.

185
00:11:45.055 --> 00:11:49.181
Grant the test app the broad
directory write permission on purpose,

186
00:11:49.181 --> 00:11:52.055
sweep it, and record the
owner's remove decision.

187
00:11:52.055 --> 00:11:54.593
A second person approves the plan's hash.

188
00:11:54.593 --> 00:11:57.648
Then dry-run it and apply
it, and confirm on the next

189
00:11:57.648 --> 00:12:00.555
sweep and in the audit log
that the grant is gone.

190
00:12:00.555 --> 00:12:05.535
To back it out, step six grants the removed
permissions again with Learn's grant

191
00:12:05.535 --> 00:12:08.217
request, removes the
test identity's roles,

192
00:12:08.217 --> 00:12:11.205
and deletes the identity,
the app and the output.

193
00:12:11.205 --> 00:12:13.720
The authors haven't run Lab 2.

194
00:12:17.470 --> 00:12:18.857
The federal requirement.

195
00:12:18.857 --> 00:12:24.305
Gate 4 meets NIST special publication
eight hundred fifty-three A C six,

196
00:12:24.305 --> 00:12:26.983
A C six enhancement five, and I A nine.

197
00:12:26.983 --> 00:12:30.382
The module's table maps each
control to its evidence.

198
00:12:30.382 --> 00:12:33.671
For least privilege, it's
the sweep and the findings.

199
00:12:33.671 --> 00:12:36.395
For privileged accounts,
the hand-off file.

200
00:12:36.395 --> 00:12:40.245
For review of privileges, the
owner review and its status.

201
00:12:40.245 --> 00:12:43.000
For change control, the plan and its hash,

202
00:12:43.000 --> 00:12:47.008
and the results and manifest
from the dry run and the apply.

203
00:12:47.008 --> 00:12:51.395
For separation of duties, the plan's
hash approved by a second person.

204
00:12:51.395 --> 00:12:55.432
And for the run's integrity, the
manifest in each output folder.

205
00:12:55.432 --> 00:13:00.071
K S I's are FedRAMP twenty X indicators
for cloud service providers.

206
00:13:00.071 --> 00:13:03.755
The agency lines its evidence
up with what its providers

207
00:13:03.755 --> 00:13:06.970
report against them; it
doesn't report K S I's itself.

208
00:13:06.970 --> 00:13:10.570
Which file supports which
indicator is the program's mapping.

209
00:13:14.320 --> 00:13:16.295
Check yourself with two questions.

210
00:13:16.295 --> 00:13:20.707
First: Deploy Bot holds application
read-write and a directory role.

211
00:13:20.707 --> 00:13:22.270
What does the sweep do with it?

212
00:13:22.270 --> 00:13:23.495
The answer is b.

213
00:13:23.495 --> 00:13:26.753
It hands it whole to the
Privileged Access plan,

214
00:13:26.753 --> 00:13:30.345
as N P L P oh eight, and leaves
it out of the owner review.

215
00:13:30.345 --> 00:13:35.332
A directory role or an escalation permission
is enough to hand an identity off.

216
00:13:35.332 --> 00:13:38.794
Second: who sends the plan's
writes in a live run?

217
00:13:38.794 --> 00:13:40.371
The answer is b: a person,

218
00:13:40.371 --> 00:13:45.183
signed in with Privileged Role
Administrator, in a delegated session.

219
00:13:45.183 --> 00:13:49.584
The Microsoft Graph permissions reference
cautions that the write permissions can

220
00:13:49.584 --> 00:13:51.320
grant authorization themselves,

221
00:13:51.320 --> 00:13:54.233
so they're never an unattended
app's standing permission.

222
00:13:54.233 --> 00:13:56.659
The module page has three more questions,

223
00:13:56.659 --> 00:13:59.520
each linked to the
section that answers it.

224
00:14:03.270 --> 00:14:04.095
To sum up.

225
00:14:04.095 --> 00:14:07.274
You swept eleven service
principals and twenty grants,

226
00:14:07.274 --> 00:14:10.108
found the broad grants
with no current exception,

227
00:14:10.108 --> 00:14:14.670
and handed three privileged service
principals to the Privileged Access plan.

228
00:14:14.670 --> 00:14:19.269
You read the open rows of the quarter's
owner review, applied three approved

229
00:14:19.269 --> 00:14:23.645
changes to the fake, and saw the write
path refuse a plan nobody reviewed.

230
00:14:23.645 --> 00:14:29.062
The evidence an ISSO keeps for Gate 4 from
this module is the sweep, the findings,

231
00:14:29.062 --> 00:14:34.228
the hand-off, the owner review and its
status, the plan with its approved hash,

232
00:14:34.228 --> 00:14:37.895
